---
// The research narrative, organised as a field-level design-space synthesis rather than as an
// argument for one hypothesis.
//
// Everything structural on this page is rendered from the artifacts in research/design-space/
// and research/knowledge/, so the page cannot assert a position the YAML does not hold. The
// previously proposed phase + methylation direction appears once, as G2 among five candidate
// opportunities, with no privileged status — which is why the page no longer needs a parked
// banner: the demotion is structural rather than a note on top of an unchanged argument.
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import ResearchLayout from "@layouts/ResearchLayout.astro";
import ResearchSection from "@components/ResearchSection.astro";
import CitationMarker from "@components/CitationMarker.astro";
import ReferenceList from "@components/ReferenceList.astro";
import KeyMessage from "@components/KeyMessage.astro";
import MechanismFigure from "@components/MechanismFigure.astro";
import { siteMeta } from "@data/siteMeta";
import { candidateLandscape } from "@data/week001";

const summary = candidateLandscape.summary;
const totalCalls = summary.total_calls.toLocaleString("en-US");
const passSnvs = summary.pass_snvs.toLocaleString("en-US");

const axes = (await getCollection("designAxes")).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
const methods = await getCollection("designMethods");
const gaps = await getCollection("designGaps");
const capabilities = await getCollection("ontCapabilities");

const reviewed = methods.filter((m) => m.data.in_reviewed_set);
const detectors = reviewed.filter((m) => m.data.is_detector);

// Reference keys by their IEEE number, so a method's `ref` resolves to the site's citation key.
const refKeyByNumber = [
  "",
  "mrdetect",
  "mrd-edge",
  "phased-seq",
  "ont-realtime",
  "nanorcs",
  "methyl-fragmentation",
  "ont-cfdna-methyl",
  "clairs-to"
];

// The matrix is wide; these axes go in the table and the rest live in the axis cards above it.
const matrixAxes = [
  "marker-discovery",
  "evidence-per-observation",
  "background-suppression",
  "evidence-linkage",
  "aggregation",
  "detection-unit",
  "decision-threshold"
];
const axisName = (id: string) => axes.find((a) => a.id === id)?.data.name ?? id;

const statusOrder = ["AVAILABLE", "DERIVABLE", "UNKNOWN", "MISSING"] as const;
const capabilityGroups = statusOrder.map((status) => ({
  status,
  note: {
    AVAILABLE: "In the BAMs now, checked by reading them",
    DERIVABLE: "Obtainable here by a named path that has not been run",
    UNKNOWN: "Cannot be stated without a check or an answer",
    MISSING: "Not obtainable from this data, for a stated reason"
  }[status],
  items: capabilities.filter((c) => c.data.status === status)
}));
---

<ResearchLayout title={siteMeta.title} description={siteMeta.subtitle}>
  <ResearchSection
    id="overview"
    index="01"
    kicker="Field synthesis"
    title={siteMeta.title}
    lede={siteMeta.subtitle}
  >
    <p>
      This page reads the MRD literature as a design space rather than as a sequence of papers.
      Each reviewed method is a set of choices along the same axes; the useful question is which
      combinations of choices the reviewed set does <em>not</em> contain, and which of those this
      project's data could actually test.
    </p>

    <div class="overview-grid">
      <article class="overview-card">
        <h3>What is compared</h3>
        <p>
          {reviewed.length} reviewed documents — {detectors.length} detection methods, and two that
          constrain rather than detect — positioned on {axes.length} axes.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="overview-card">
        <h3>What is measured</h3>
        <p>
          Platform capabilities are inventoried against the actual BAMs, not against what ONT
          supports in principle: {capabilityGroups[0].items.length} available,
          {" "}{capabilityGroups[1].items.length} derivable, {capabilityGroups[2].items.length} unknown,
          {" "}{capabilityGroups[3].items.length} missing.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="overview-card">
        <h3>What is proposed</h3>
        <p>
          Nothing yet. {gaps.length} candidate opportunities are recorded with their falsification
          conditions; none is selected, and this project's previous direction is one of them.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="overview-card">
        <h3>Local starting point</h3>
        <p>
          Tumor-only calling <CitationMarker refs="clairs-to" /> produced {totalCalls} candidate
          records, of which {passSnvs} are PASS SNV candidates — a caller retention label, not
          confirmed somatic truth.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <KeyMessage title="What “underexplored” means on this page" tone="boundary">
      <p>
        Underexplored means <strong>not represented in the seven documents reviewed here</strong>. A
        systematic literature search has not been run, so nothing on this page is a claim about the
        field as a whole, and nothing is described as novel. Method positions derive from indexed
        summaries rather than from source PDFs read locally.
      </p>
    </KeyMessage>
  </ResearchSection>

  <ResearchSection
    id="mrd-problem"
    index="02"
    kicker="The problem"
    title="A rare signal in an identical background"
  >
    <p>
      <strong>Minimal — or measurable — residual disease</strong> is the small amount of tumor that
      can persist after treatment while remaining invisible to imaging. Detecting it matters because
      it is the earliest available evidence about the outcome of treatment.
    </p>
    <p>
      The difficulty is proportion. Tumor-derived molecules are heavily outnumbered by molecules
      from normal tissue that are chemically identical to them. Three constraints follow, and every
      method below is a response to them: tumor-derived molecules are <strong>few</strong>, so any
      single locus carries little evidence; sequencing errors <strong>imitate</strong> true
      mutations; and a molecule described by a single fact cannot be told apart from a background
      molecule described by the same fact.
    </p>
    <p class="display-ratio">
      <span class="display-ratio__num">Tumor signal</span>
      <span class="display-ratio__bar" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="display-ratio__den">Background noise</span>
    </p>
    <p>
      Background is more than sequencing error. In a tumor-only setting it also contains germline
      variation with no matched normal to subtract it, clonal haematopoiesis, alignment artifacts,
      and — for methylation specifically — bias introduced during library preparation
      <CitationMarker refs="ont-cfdna-methyl" />.
    </p>
  </ResearchSection>

  <ResearchSection
    id="design-space"
    index="03"
    kicker="Taxonomy"
    title="The detector design space"
    lede="Eleven axes along which a method makes a choice. Every reviewed method is a position on each."
  >
    <KeyMessage title="Three things that are not the same axis" tone="message">
      <p>
        <strong>Signal enrichment</strong> selects which observations are worth aggregating.
        <strong>Background suppression</strong> stops a non-tumor observation from imitating a tumor
        one. <strong>Aggregation</strong> combines many weak observations into one number. They act
        at different points, and conflating them is the most common way this space is misread.
      </p>
    </KeyMessage>

    <div class="axis-list">
      {axes.map((axis) => (
        <article class="axis-card" id={`axis-${axis.id}`}>
          <header class="axis-card__head">
            <span class="axis-card__number">{String(axis.data.order).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
            <h3>{axis.data.name}</h3>
            {axis.data.added && <span class="axis-card__tag">added axis</span>}
          </header>
          <p class="axis-card__definition">{axis.data.definition}</p>
          <p class="axis-card__why"><strong>Why it matters.</strong> {axis.data.why_it_matters}</p>
          {axis.data.justification && (
            <p class="axis-card__why"><strong>Why it is an axis.</strong> {axis.data.justification}</p>
          )}
          <ul class="axis-card__strategies">
            {axis.data.strategies.map((strategy) => (
              <li>
                <span class="axis-card__strategy">{strategy.name}</span>
                {strategy.detail && <span class="axis-card__detail">{strategy.detail}</span>}
                {strategy.methods.length > 0 && (
                  <span class="axis-card__methods">{strategy.methods.join(" · ")}</span>
                )}
                {strategy.cost && <span class="axis-card__caveat">{strategy.cost}</span>}
                {strategy.note && <span class="axis-card__caveat">{strategy.note}</span>}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          {(axis.data.open_position || axis.data.note || axis.data.tension || axis.data.constraint) && (
            <p class="axis-card__open">
              {axis.data.open_position ?? axis.data.note ?? axis.data.tension ?? axis.data.constraint}
            </p>
          )}
          {axis.data.measured_locally && (
            <p class="axis-card__measured">Measured on this data — {axis.data.measured_locally}</p>
          )}
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </ResearchSection>

  <ResearchSection
    id="common-principles"
    index="04"
    kicker="Synthesis A"
    title="Common principles"
    lede="Listed only where at least three reviewed methods rest on the same idea."
  >
    <div class="principle-grid">
      <article class="principle">
        <h3>P1 · Make each observation carry more before combining</h3>
        <p>
          Present in all five detectors, which differ almost entirely in <em>how</em>: more loci
          <CitationMarker refs="mrdetect" />, a learned prior <CitationMarker refs="mrd-edge" />, a
          second linked variant <CitationMarker refs="phased-seq" />, a physical consensus
          <CitationMarker refs="nanorcs" />, a second signal class <CitationMarker refs="ont-realtime" />.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="principle">
        <h3>P2 · Background is modelled, not merely filtered</h3>
        <p>
          Nobody in the set treats a filter list as sufficient: the error distribution is modelled
          <CitationMarker refs="mrdetect" />, learned <CitationMarker refs="mrd-edge" />, required to
          fail twice <CitationMarker refs="phased-seq" />, or constrained at the bench
          <CitationMarker refs="ont-cfdna-methyl" />.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="principle">
        <h3>P3 · The sample answer is a scalar over many weak observations</h3>
        <p>
          The unit at which evidence is gathered differs across methods; the unit at which it is
          reported does not <CitationMarker refs={["mrdetect", "mrd-edge", "nanorcs"]} />.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="principle">
        <h3>P4 · Evidence classes are not automatically independent</h3>
        <p>
          Held explicitly by the fragmentation work <CitationMarker refs="methyl-fragmentation" />,
          and implicitly violated by the multimodal methods, which combine channels without testing
          independence.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </ResearchSection>

  <ResearchSection
    id="unique-mechanisms"
    index="05"
    kicker="Synthesis B"
    title="Unique mechanisms"
    lede="What only one method in the reviewed set does — the reason it exists, not a summary of it."
  >
    <div class="mechanism-list">
      {reviewed.map((method) => (
        <article class="mechanism-row">
          <div class="mechanism-row__name">
            <strong>{method.data.name}</strong>
            {method.data.ref && <CitationMarker refs={refKeyByNumber[method.data.ref]} />}
            {!method.data.is_detector && <span class="mechanism-row__tag">not a detector</span>}
          </div>
          <div class="mechanism-row__body">
            <p>{method.data.unique_contribution}</p>
            <p class="mechanism-row__limit">
              <strong>Limitation for a tumor-only long-read setting.</strong> {method.data.limitation_here}
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </ResearchSection>

  <ResearchSection
    id="method-matrix"
    index="06"
    kicker="Comparison"
    title="Method × axis"
    lede="Each method's position on seven of the eleven axes. Read down a column to see how crowded an axis is."
  >
    <div class="matrix-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Method by axis comparison" tabindex="0">
      <table class="axis-matrix">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Method</th>
            {matrixAxes.map((id) => <th scope="col">{axisName(id)}</th>)}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {methods.map((method) => (
            <tr class={method.data.in_reviewed_set ? undefined : "axis-matrix__row--tooling"}>
              <th scope="row">
                {method.data.name}
                {!method.data.in_reviewed_set && <span class="axis-matrix__tag">local tooling</span>}
              </th>
              {matrixAxes.map((id) => <td>{method.data.axes[id] ?? "—"}</td>)}
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="figure-note">
      Positions derive from indexed summaries, not from source PDFs read locally. No performance
      figure appears anywhere on this page: the studies use different assays, sample types and
      cohorts, so their numbers are not comparable.
    </p>
  </ResearchSection>

  <ResearchSection
    id="unresolved"
    index="07"
    kicker="Open"
    title="Unresolved challenges"
    lede="Problems the reviewed set names but does not settle."
  >
    <ul class="synthesis-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Tumor-naive detection.</strong> Every reviewed detector is tumor-informed. Without a
        prior tumor profile the marker set carries its own false-positive structure, and no reviewed
        method addresses that.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Modality independence is assumed, not tested.</strong> Two methods combine channels;
        one paper shows two of those channels are coupled <CitationMarker refs="methyl-fragmentation" />.
        Nothing in the set resolves the conflict with an ablation.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Linkage reach is a platform property nobody spends.</strong> Long reads co-observe
        everything within a read by construction, and both long-read methods use that for
        multimodality and accuracy rather than for background suppression
        <CitationMarker refs={["ont-realtime", "nanorcs"]} />.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Accuracy and modality pull against each other.</strong> Consensus buys per-read
        confidence through amplification <CitationMarker refs="nanorcs" />; native sequencing keeps
        base modifications and accepts the error rate <CitationMarker refs="ont-cfdna-methyl" />.
        Nothing measures what each choice costs on the same material.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Validation frameworks do not transfer.</strong> The only analytical-validation
        framing in the set is short-read, targeted and tumor-informed
        <CitationMarker refs="phased-seq" />.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </ResearchSection>

  <ResearchSection
    id="ont-capabilities"
    index="08"
    kicker="Platform"
    title="ONT capability space"
    lede="What this data provides — checked against the BAMs, not against what the platform supports in principle."
  >
    <div class="capability-groups">
      {capabilityGroups.map((group) => (
        <article class={`capability-group capability-group--${group.status.toLowerCase()}`}>
          <header>
            <h3>{group.status}</h3>
            <p>{group.note}</p>
          </header>
          <ul>
            {group.items.map((item) => (
              <li>
                <span class="capability__name">{item.data.capability}</span>
                <span class="capability__evidence">{item.data.evidence}</span>
                {item.data.path && <span class="capability__path">Route — {item.data.path}</span>}
                {item.data.caveat && <span class="capability__caveat">{item.data.caveat}</span>}
                {item.data.consequence && <span class="capability__caveat">{item.data.consequence}</span>}
                {item.data.restriction && <span class="capability__caveat">{item.data.restriction}</span>}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </ResearchSection>

  <ResearchSection
    id="resources"
    index="09"
    kicker="Materials"
    title="Available experimental resources"
    lede="What can be run, with what, against what. Source data is read-only throughout."
  >
    <div class="resource-grid">
      <article class="resource-card">
        <h3>Material</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>HCC1395 tumor, ONT with native modification calls — candidate discovery</li>
          <li>HCC1395BL normal — <strong>evaluation only</strong>, never an inference input</li>
          <li>Dilution series at ~25×: <strong>5 blanks</strong>, plus 3 replicates each at 1%, 0.1% and 0.01%</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="resource-card">
        <h3>Truth and background</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>SEQC2 high-confidence somatic calls and regions — evaluation only</li>
          <li>Orthogonal-tools benchmark — evaluation only</li>
          <li>Population and panel-of-normals databases, including a long-read population set</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="resource-card">
        <h3>Tooling</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>LongPhase 2.0.2 — present but unrun; needs a heterozygous variant set first</li>
          <li>ClairS-TO v0.5.0 — produced the candidate set; its wrapper currently does not start</li>
          <li>112 cores, 503 GB RAM, <strong>no GPU</strong></li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="resource-card">
        <h3>Blocking unknowns</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>The basecaller model is not recorded in the BAM header</li>
          <li>Phase block length at 25× is unmeasured</li>
          <li>Composition of the candidate set is unknown</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="figure-note">
      The five blank replicates are the most consequential item here: they are what makes a
      blank-anchored decision rule possible at all.
    </p>
  </ResearchSection>

  <ResearchSection
    id="explored"
    index="10"
    kicker="Coverage"
    title="Explored vs underexplored"
    lede="Within the reviewed set only. A systematic search has not been run."
  >
    <div class="coverage-grid">
      <article class="coverage coverage--well">
        <h3>Well explored</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Genome-wide aggregation of weak per-locus SNV evidence, tumor-informed</li>
          <li>Learned background suppression on short-read sequence context</li>
          <li>Linkage-based suppression within a short cfDNA fragment</li>
          <li>Physical accuracy improvement by consensus on long reads</li>
          <li>Native multi-class readout from one ONT library, at region scale</li>
          <li>Analytical validation framing — limit of blank, limit of detection, precision</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="coverage coverage--partial">
        <h3>Partially explored</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Long reads used for multimodality, but not for linkage-based background suppression</li>
          <li>Multimodal aggregation with modality independence assumed rather than tested</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="coverage coverage--infeasible">
        <h3>Not testable on this data</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Fragmentomics</strong> — this is cell-line genomic DNA, not cfDNA: fragment ends reflect library preparation, not nuclease biology</li>
          <li><strong>Consensus-accuracy designs</strong> — the data is simplex, with no amplification-based consensus</li>
          <li><strong>Any clinical MRD performance claim</strong> — no cohort, and dilution material is not plasma</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </ResearchSection>

  <ResearchSection
    id="opportunities"
    index="11"
    kicker="Candidates"
    title="Candidate research opportunities"
    lede="Five candidates, each with the condition that would kill it. None is selected."
  >
    <div class="gap-list">
      {gaps.map((gap) => (
        <article class="gap-card" id={`gap-${gap.id.toLowerCase()}`}>
          <header class="gap-card__head">
            <span class="gap-card__id">{gap.id}</span>
            <h3>{gap.data.title}</h3>
            <span class={`gap-card__status gap-card__status--${gap.data.status.split("-")[0]}`}>
              {gap.data.status.replace(/-/g, " ")}
            </span>
          </header>
          <p class="gap-card__axes">
            {gap.data.axes_combined.map((id) => <span class="gap-card__axis">{axisName(id)}</span>)}
          </p>
          <dl class="gap-card__fields">
            <dt>What is actually different</dt>
            <dd>{gap.data.what_is_actually_different}</dd>
            <dt>Closest existing methods</dt>
            <dd>
              {gap.data.closest_methods.length > 0
                ? gap.data.closest_methods
                    .map((id) => methods.find((m) => m.id === id)?.data.name ?? id)
                    .join(", ")
                : "None in the reviewed set"}
            </dd>
            <dt>Feasibility here</dt>
            <dd>{gap.data.feasibility}</dd>
            <dt>Confounding risks</dt>
            <dd>
              <ul>{gap.data.confounding_risks.map((risk) => <li>{risk}</li>)}</ul>
            </dd>
            <dt>How it would be falsified</dt>
            <dd class="gap-card__falsify">{gap.data.falsification}</dd>
            {gap.data.evidence.length > 0 && (
              <>
                <dt>Evidence so far</dt>
                <dd class="gap-card__evidence">{gap.data.evidence.join(" · ")}</dd>
              </>
            )}
          </dl>
          {gap.data.note && <p class="gap-card__note">{gap.data.note}</p>}
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

    <h3 class="figure-heading">Mechanism sketch for G2, the previously proposed direction</h3>
    <p>
      G2 is the direction this project pursued before the synthesis. The figure is kept because the
      mechanistic argument is worth reading; the direction is demoted because it is one candidate
      among five rather than the answer.
    </p>
    <MechanismFigure>
      <strong>Mechanistic rationale for one candidate, not a proposal.</strong> A somatic variant
      arises on one chromosome copy, so a tumor-derived read should carry it in cis with the same
      neighbouring alleles every time; an error is free to land on a read from either haplotype, and
      it does not alter that molecule&#39;s methylation. Whether that separates anything at low
      tumor fraction is unmeasured — see G2's falsification condition above.
    </MechanismFigure>

    <KeyMessage title="Nothing here is selected" tone="boundary">
      <p>
        These are candidates, ordered by identifier and not by preference. Choosing among them needs
        the composition of the candidate set (G5), phase block length at 25×, and joint availability
        per molecule — none of which has been measured. That selection is a researcher's decision,
        not one this synthesis makes.
      </p>
    </KeyMessage>
  </ResearchSection>

  <ResearchSection id="references" index="12" kicker="Bibliography" title="References">
    <ReferenceList />
  </ResearchSection>
</ResearchLayout>
